ther a  control sso or one of these  sso1 or ss2 sso2 so splice switching  oligo we call them  we can see when we transact with the  control oligo on the wild side nothing  happens that's the same for the ss01 and  sso2  but in the mutant uh here we have the  control where we have predominant  skipping just as before  of the exon when we treat with either of  these sso  we get predominant inclusion so so it  seems like we are predict  we are affecting binding and we removing  the negative effect of tdp-43  so this is a case of where the binding  profile actually is  is fairly essential to actually  designing these therapeutics  which because this is an intronic  variation  the patient would when treated with  these oligos would produce a completely  normal function and a  functional enzyme and not have disease  so uh these because these binding  profiles they  they sort of uh also show patterns of  binding  uh and because tdp 43 is is this  known factor involved in in amiotrophic  lateral sclerosis  als where it's uh often observed to  either be mutated itself and causing  familial  als or it's depleted from the nucleus  and and sort of sequestered in the  cytoplasm and that way it's not able to  exert its normally from  normal function in the nucleus and gp43  is known to repress  pseudo exons so as i mentioned before  pseudo accidents are part of the introns  that are not supposed to normally be  included  and part of that is because they're  repressed by splicing inhibitors  and one of those might be tb-43 in some  cases  so uh losing tdp-43 in the new from the  nucleus  could activate pseudo excellence and be  a part of what drives the  als pathology so there's been a number  of studies so here's one by gin  colleagues  from 2017 where they were looking at  pseudoexons that are specifically  activated  either in muscle and neuron by  specifically knocking down tdp-43  so we took those pseudo-exons and then  we  produced binding profiles across them  and then we 